<p><strong>How to lower cortisol levels depends on why they are high.</strong></p>
<p>Good sleep, regular movement and relaxation can help you manage everyday stress. A diagnosed cortisol disorder needs medical care aimed at its cause.</p>
<p>Feeling stressed doesn't prove your cortisol is high. And lower cortisol is not always better.</p>
<p>This guide is from Cortisol+, a wellness app for wearables. The app does not measure cortisol or treat hormone disorders. We keep two things apart: changes in how you feel, and changes a hormone test has confirmed.</p>

<h2>How to lower cortisol levels naturally</h2>
<p><strong>Start with habits that help sleep and stress.</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Make time for sleep.</li>
  <li>Keep a routine you can stick to.</li>
  <li>Move regularly, at a level that suits you.</li>
  <li>Try a relaxation practice that feels comfortable.</li>
</ul>
<p>Write down what you do and how you feel. These habits can be worth it even if they don't prove your cortisol fell.</p>
<p>If a test result is abnormal or symptoms don't go away, ask a clinician what it means before trying to lower a hormone yourself.</p>

