<h2>1. Make sleep a habit you can repeat</h2>
<p><strong>Steady bed and wake times are a good place to start.</strong></p>
<p><a href="https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/sleep-deprivation/healthy-sleep-habits">NHLBI's healthy-sleep guidance</a> recommends making time for sleep, keeping steady bed and wake times, and having a quiet wind-down before bed. It also talks about caffeine and alcohol near bedtime.</p>
<p>Pick one change that fits your life. You don't need a strict online "cortisol reset".</p>
<p>Write down bedtime, wake time and how rested you feel in our <a href="/resources/stress-sleep-journal/">free sleep and stress journal</a>.</p>
<p>If sleep problems keep going, get advice about the sleep problem itself. Don't assume cortisol is the cause.</p>

<ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen_new_tour_sleep.webp">
  Change <strong>one thing</strong> at a time, like a set wake-up time. Then you can tell what actually helped.
</ZenTip>

<h2>2. Use relaxation to manage stress</h2>
<p><strong>A short, comfortable practice is the goal.</strong></p>
<p><a href="https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/stress">NCCIH summarizes the evidence for relaxation, mindfulness and slow breathing</a>. Some studies report changes in cortisol, but results vary by practice, study and group of people.</p>
<p>That evidence doesn't support promising an exact percentage drop. It also doesn't support a guaranteed effect within minutes for any one person.</p>
<p>Don't force long breath holds. Stop any technique that makes you dizzy or upset. You're looking for a way to manage stress you can keep up, not a breathing high score.</p>

<h2>3. Choose movement you can keep up, and recover</h2>
<p><strong>Regular activity can be part of a stress routine.</strong></p>
<p>Cleveland Clinic's <a href="https://health.clevelandclinic.org/how-to-reduce-cortisol-and-turn-down-the-dial-on-stress">clinician-led overview</a> talks about exercise alongside sleep and relaxation.</p>
<p>The right activity depends on your health, what you like and how you recover. This page doesn't give one workout amount for everyone to control a hormone.</p>
<p>Your wearable can show your activity and recovery if that helps. But a change in heart rate variability (HRV) is not proof that a workout lowered or raised your cortisol.</p>

<h2>4. Look at the habits around your stress</h2>
<p><strong>Notice what tends to come with bad nights or busy days.</strong></p>
<p>Do caffeine timing, alcohol, work demands or an irregular schedule show up alongside poor sleep or feeling overwhelmed? Choose one small change and write down how it goes.</p>
<p>If stress is hard to cope with or isn't getting better, professional support can help. <a href="https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/stress">NCCIH explains when to seek more help</a>.</p>
<p>For a wearable example, read <a href="/blog/alcohol-hrv-sleep/">alcohol and overnight HRV</a>. Seeing two things together doesn't prove one caused the other.</p>

<h2>Do supplements lower cortisol?</h2>
<p><strong>Some have been studied, but "natural" doesn't mean needed or risk-free.</strong></p>
<p><a href="https://www.nccih.nih.gov/health/ashwagandha">NCCIH's ashwagandha guidance</a> describes limited evidence, possible interactions and reports of liver injury.</p>
<p>Don't start a supplement just because an app score is high. Talk it through with a qualified professional, especially if you take medicine or have a health condition.</p>

<ZenPicture src="/app-ui/start/zen_explain.webp" caption="Feeling less stressed and having lower cortisol are two different results." />

<h2 id="evidence">What does the research actually measure?</h2>
<p><strong>Evidence that people felt less stressed is not automatically evidence of lower cortisol.</strong> Check what a study measured before you apply it to your routine.</p>

<div class="not-prose my-6 grid gap-3">
